# Arithva does not need any API key to run.
#
# Speech recognition and speech synthesis use the browser's Web Speech API, and
# intent routing is rule-based, so the demo works offline apart from the weather
# call. This is deliberate: a live demo should not be able to fail because a
# model endpoint is slow or a key expired.
#
# The optional settings below are for later work.

# Where the frontend should reach the API. Empty means same origin.
# ARITHVA_API_BASE=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- VOICE
#
# Spoken replies try each provider in turn:
#
#     ElevenLabs  ->  Amazon Polly  ->  the browser's own voice
#
# Set none of these and Arithva still works, speaking with the browser voice.
# Set one or both and it uses them, falling through on any failure. Copy this
# file to .env to fill them in. NEVER commit .env.

# ElevenLabs
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
#
# Optional. Defaults are in backend/tools/speech_tool.py. VERIFY the voice and
# model in your dashboard, and listen to a sample in each language you plan to
# demo before relying on them.
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=
# ELEVENLABS_MODEL_ID=eleven_multilingual_v2

# Amazon Polly. Needs: pip install boto3
# Use an IAM user limited to polly:SynthesizeSpeech, not your root keys.
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_REGION=ap-south-1
#
# Optional. Kajal is Polly's Indian-English and Hindi neural voice. Whether
# Polly covers Kannada is UNVERIFIED — check the AWS docs for your region.
# AWS_POLLY_VOICE_ID=Kajal
# AWS_POLLY_ENGINE=neural

# Optional: a hosted speech model for better Kannada recognition than the
# browser's. Not wired up yet.
# BHASHINI_API_KEY=
# BHASHINI_USER_ID=

# Optional: an LLM for intent routing on sentences the keyword router misses.
# The model would still never produce a number; tools do that.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
